    "interpretation": "=== DISPOSITION EFFECT SIMULATION VALIDATION: VALID ===\nOverall Fit Score: 100.0% (threshold: 50%)\n\n[1] PROPORTION OF GAINS REALIZED (PGR) ANALYSIS\n    Observed: PGR = 0.4881\n    Expected: PGR > 0.20 (investors sell winners)\n    Score: 100.0%\n\n    Interpretation:\n    PGR measures: (Realized Gains) / (Realized Gains + Paper Gains)\n    HIGH PGR - Investors frequently realize gains.\n    Strong evidence of 'selling winners too early' behavior.\n    Prospect theory explains this: risk-averse in gains,\n    investors lock in profits to avoid potential loss.\n\n[2] PGR vs PLR ASYMMETRY (Core Disposition Test)\n    Observed: PGR = 0.4881, PLR = 0.1447\n    Expected: PGR > PLR (sell winners, hold losers)\n    Score: 100.0%\n\n    Interpretation:\n    The disposition effect = systematic tendency to:\n    - Sell winners too early (high PGR)\n    - Hold losers too long (low PLR)\n    DISPOSITION CONFIRMED - PGR/PLR ratio = 3.37\n    Investors are more likely to realize gains than losses.\n    This matches Kahneman-Tversky prospect theory:\n    - Risk-averse in gains (sell to lock in profit)\n    - Risk-seeking in losses (hold hoping for recovery)\n\n[3] DISPOSITION COEFFICIENT (PGR - PLR)\n    Observed: Coefficient = 0.3434\n    Expected: > 0.05 (meaningful disposition effect)\n    Score: 100.0%\n\n    Interpretation:\n    STRONG EFFECT - Large disposition coefficient.\n    The asymmetry between gain/loss realization is pronounced.\n    This exceeds typical empirical findings and suggests\n    agents exhibit strong loss aversion (\u03bb >> 2).\n\n[SUMMARY]\nThe simulation successfully reproduces disposition effect:\n- PGR > PLR (sell winners, hold losers)\n- Positive disposition coefficient\n- Results align with Shefrin-Statman (1985) and Odean (1998)."
